---
import SectionHead from './SectionHead.astro';

const P = 'text-body text-ink-2 [text-wrap:pretty]';
const EYEBROW = 'mb-3.5 text-meta font-medium tracking-[0.2em] uppercase text-muted';
const SIDE = 'mt-[clamp(32px,4vw,52px)] border-t border-rule pt-[22px]';
---

<section
  id="about"
  aria-labelledby="h-about"
  class="mx-auto max-w-shell px-gut py-[clamp(80px,12vw,160px)]"
>
  <SectionHead num="01" id="h-about" label="About" />

  <div
    class="grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.55fr)_minmax(0,1fr)] items-start
           gap-[clamp(44px,8vw,120px)] max-xl:grid-cols-1"
  >
    <div class="reveal">
      <p class={`dropcap ${P}`}>
        I care less about which framework is on the page than whether the thing works for
        the person using it. Most of my career has been spent on products where that
        distinction matters — tools where a wrong number, a buried control, or an
        unreadable table has a real cost on the other side of the screen.
      </p>
      <p class={`mt-[22px] ${P}`}>
        At ChargePoint I led front-end architecture for the Fleet Charging platform.
        Charging is a scheduling problem wearing a hardware costume: limited power,
        competing vehicles, tariffs with seasonal and critical-peak rules, equipment that
        fails at inconvenient moments. Real-time updates and intuitive thoughtful UX make the difference between not knowing what is happening and running a tight ship.
      </p>
      <p class={`mt-[22px] ${P}`}>
        Before that I was a lead on Tesla's vehicle configurator team: we were responsible for the discovery and
        configuration flows customers used across 40-plus markets. Different
        domain, same shape — a great many constraints underneath, and an interface that has
        to feel simple even though it was anything but.
      </p>
      <p class={`mt-[22px] ${P}`}>
        I work across the stack when it helps, spend real time in Figma, and treat
        accessibility as part of the definition of done rather than a late audit. I build
        with AI in the loop daily — spec-first, brainstorming, prototyping, refactoring.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="reveal">
      <p
        class="border-l-2 border-accent pl-6 font-display text-pull italic
               tracking-[-0.016em] text-ink [text-wrap:balance]"
      >
        The interface is where a system either earns someone's trust or loses it.
      </p>

      <div class={SIDE}>
        <p class={EYEBROW}>What I bring</p>
        <p class="text-caption text-ink-3 [text-wrap:pretty]">
          Twenty years of shipped front end. Comfort with genuinely complicated domains —
          energy, automotive, healthcare, commerce. A design sensibility that came from
          being a real part of the design process, not just executing.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/*
        Trimmed to a single orienting line. Spec §14 moves detailed
        technology lists to the résumé — the mock carried three
        separate inventories on the homepage, which is the toolkit
        section the redesign set out to remove.
      */}
      <div class={SIDE}>
        <p class={EYEBROW}>Mostly working in</p>
        <p class="text-caption text-ink-3 [text-wrap:pretty]">
          React · TypeScript · Node · Tailwind · Figma. The full list, and the twenty years
          of it, lives on the <a class="ul-draw text-accent hover:text-accent-strong" href="#cv">CV</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>
